  "scenario": "A user has been told by their doctor to cut caffeine way back but wants to preserve a satisfying morning-coffee and afternoon-tea ritual and dislikes watery decaf. Four frozen seller offers are available: a 17.49-dollar 12-ounce whole-bean Merry Mocha Mint product titled SWP Decaf whose page claims Swiss Water processing and 99.9-percent caffeine-free while protecting flavor; a 6.99-dollar Seattle's Best Decaf Portside ground-coffee offer with a 78-percent-of-100 aggregate over twelve reviews whose title and quick-look say 12 ounces but whose product-dimensions line says 10.4 ounces; a 27-dollar 100-gram Ocha & Co. hojicha powder seller-labeled low caffeine with a sweet smoky taste; and a 22.96-dollar six-pack of 18-bag Good Earth decaffeinated lemongrass green tea with a 92-percent-of-100 aggregate over twelve reviews. None of the four frozen pages supplies independently verified caffeine milligrams for the exact prepared serving. General pages distinguish caffeine reduction from zero caffeine, describe method and flavor-compound tradeoffs, and show that hojicha, white tea, and herbal infusions cannot be ranked by a light, mellow, or category label alone. Three community pages contain one preparation-change anecdote, one question and answer about lighter-looking less-bitter lemon tea, and one small-sample loose-tea shopping discussion. The research problem is non-diagnostic: follow the user's individualized clinician instruction, audit what is actually known, separate residual caffeine from flavor and ritual, and recommend only a reversible trial that begins after exact serving exposure is verified to fit the clinician's limit.",
