    "decaf_low_caffeine_offer_disclosure_matrix_v1": "The frozen Merry Mocha Mint whole-bean page is SKU B07KC6D3HR at 17.49 dollars and the title says 12 ounces and SWP Decaf; the seller also claims Swiss Water processing, 99.9-percent caffeine-free, and flavor protection. The frozen Seattle's Best Decaf Portside ground-coffee page is SKU B01LTI9656 at 6.99 dollars with a 78-percent-of-100 aggregate over twelve reviews; its title and quick-look say 12 ounces, while its product-dimensions line says 10.4 ounces, so exact net weight must be resolved. The frozen Ocha & Co. hojicha powder page is SKU B0973D79W7 at 27 dollars and seller-labels 100 grams or 3.5 ounces as lower caffeine than regular green teas with smoky and sweet flavor. The frozen Good Earth page is SKU B09JKZHQ6V at 22.96 dollars with a 92-percent-of-100 aggregate over twelve reviews and a title describing six packs of 18 decaffeinated lemongrass green-tea bags. No frozen offer page provides independently verified caffeine milligrams for the exact prepared serving. Seller method, reduction, flavor, freshness, composition, and suitability language is not a laboratory result or individualized safety determination.",
    "decaffeination_residual_and_flavor_boundary_v1": "The general decaffeination page defines the process as caffeine removal, gives a United States reduction standard of at least 97 percent, and reports that tested decaffeinated drinks can retain caffeine. It describes solvent, supercritical-carbon-dioxide, and water methods that try to leave flavor precursors close to their original state, describes Swiss Water as using water rather than organic solvents, and reports that some volatile green-tea flavor compounds decreased or disappeared in one supercritical-carbon-dioxide comparison. These facts establish neither zero caffeine nor a universally best-tasting method for an exact product. A percent-reduction or seller process claim cannot be converted to milligrams per prepared serving without an exact baseline or verified finished-product measurement, serving size, and preparation.",
    "tea_category_caffeine_uncertainty_v1": "The general hojicha page says roasting lowers its caffeine and gives nutty, toasty, sweet, low-bitterness characteristics, but supplies no caffeine amount for the Ocha powder. The white-tea page says caffeine depends on tea type and brewing method, reports a wide less-than-10-to-more-than-50-milligram estimate range per cup, and says variability has prevented a simple variety relationship. The herbal-tea page distinguishes tisanes from Camellia sinensis and says most tisanes do not naturally contain caffeine, while also naming plant infusions that do contain caffeine or other stimulants and warning that some plant compounds can interact with medications. Therefore low, light, mild, pale, less bitter, white, or herbal cannot by itself establish an exact caffeine exposure or individualized suitability; exact ingredients and preparation matter.",
    "scoped_ritual_and_preparation_transfer_v1": "One TIFU author describes moving from roughly 20 to 60 grams of coffee per litre while keeping a two-mug habit and then reports exaggerated symptoms; this is an uncontrolled personal story without a measured caffeine dose or medical causal threshold. One AskScience thread contrasts lighter color and lower bitterness after lemon with a commenter's claim that caffeine extraction is unchanged; this is a question-and-answer exchange, not a laboratory measurement. One Boston thread asks for small amounts of varied loose teas and a commenter describes 10-to-20-gram samples and several categories; this supports low-commitment sampling, not product efficacy, safety, or population preference. Use the pages only to motivate fixed preparation, skepticism about sensory proxies, small samples, repeatability, and stop conditions.",
    "clinician_limit_first_safety_gate_v1": "The user's own clinician instruction overrides generic percentages, category averages, seller labels, review aggregates, and community experiences. This research task must not diagnose, choose a generic safe daily caffeine amount, prescribe a taper, treat withdrawal, or declare a product medically safe. Before consuming a candidate, obtain its exact serving size, intended preparation, and a verified finished-product caffeine value from the manufacturer or another authoritative product-specific source, then confirm that the planned morning-plus-afternoon ledger fits the clinician's instruction; also verify all ingredients and ask the clinician or pharmacist about relevant interactions. If the instruction is qualitative or unclear, product data are unavailable, or symptoms occur, defer consumption and contact the clinician. Never use an anecdote to reinterpret symptoms or continue a trial.",
    "reversible_ritual_preserving_trial_v1": "Only candidates that pass the clinician-limit-first safety gate may enter a taste and ritual trial. Buy or obtain the smallest available quantity; use separate fixed morning-coffee and afternoon-tea recipes with constant product mass, water volume, temperature, steep or brew time, additions, vessel, and time of day; randomize or blind candidate identity where practical; and repeat on multiple days without exceeding the verified ledger. Predeclare taste, body, aroma, aftertaste, perceived wateriness, preparation effort, ritual satisfaction, cost per acceptable serving, and waste thresholds. Record the verified caffeine value and actual number of servings rather than guessing from color, bitterness, or strength. Stop on symptoms, exposure uncertainty, or clinician-limit risk and contact the clinician rather than adapting the medical plan inside the trial.",
